{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Speak, etc., saying.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say ״דַּבְּרוּ״ and He did not rely on what He said in the first verse, ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ וְגוֹ׳ לֵאמֹר״. And if the word ״לֵאמֹר״ was not sufficient for Him, He should have said ״דַּבְּרוּ״ before saying ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה״ — for this commandment was given to Israel for all their generations, so why did He not say ״דַּבְּרוּ״ before it? Further, it is difficult that He said the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, for to whom would Israel say it?" "Or perhaps, since in this commandment of taking the lamb there are two aspects: one for good, for through it they lifted their heads and their enemies saw them executing judgment upon their gods, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 16:3); and the second, on account of the commandment itself, for it is the king's decree to do so, and whoever transgresses it will be punished. Therefore it was necessary to say <b>דַּבְּרוּ</b> etc. <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — ״דַּבְּרוּ״ is a term of hardness, corresponding to the exigency of the matter and the king's decree and to punish every transgressor; and ״לֵאמֹר״ is a soft utterance, corresponding to the preeminence and honor that they will attain through this matter. And since regarding the commandment He gave at the beginning of the prophecy — which is to count from Nisan — there is no punishment for one who transgresses it like the punishment for one who neglects to perform the Pesach, therefore He said only ״לֵאמֹר״ regarding it; and consequently it was necessary to say again ״דַּבְּרוּ״ corresponding to the commandment of Pesach, and He further said ״לֵאמֹר״ because in it too there is preeminence for Israel as mentioned. And by way of derash, He commanded them to guide Israel in the conduct of Torah and commandments, to make known to them the statutes of HaShem and His teachings and to lead them in a straight path — and therefore He said ״דַּבְּרוּ״ and He said ״לֵאמֹר,״ meaning, so that they would be preeminent, in the manner of His saying (Isaiah 3:10): ״אִמְרוּ צַדִּיק״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend that first he would speak to the elders, as we find that Moses did so, for it is written (Exodus 12:21): ״וַיִּקְרָא מֹשֶׁה לְכָל זִקְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — and that is His saying <b>דַּבְּרוּ אֶל כָּל עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל לֵאמֹר</b>, that he should say to the people; and this too is what the verse there says: ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵיהֶם.״" "<b>And they shall take for themselves, etc.</b> It is difficult: why did He say a vav at the beginning of the matter? Or perhaps it alludes to the things stated in Moses' words to Israel, for it is written ״מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ״ etc., and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 16:2): ״מִשְׁכוּ יְדֵיכֶם מֵעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה״ etc., and this matter was not stated in the words of HaShem; but from Moses' words it is heard, for thus was said to him in prophecy — and that is His saying ״וְיִקְחוּ״ with the addition of the vav, alluding that a utterance preceded it, and what it is — ״מִשְׁכוּ״ that Moses said." "Further, it may intend: since He said ״בֶּעָשׂוֹר״ — one might think: if he took it on the tenth, he may slaughter it; if he did not take it on the tenth, he may not take it on the eleventh, the twelfth, or the thirteenth — Scripture therefore says ״וְיִקְחוּ״ to include afterward as well. And in the Mekhilta (Exodus 12:3) they taught this law of taking on the eleventh, the twelfth, and the thirteenth by an a fortiori argument, and a matter derived by a fortiori reasoning, if a hint to it is found in the verse, we agree to say that this is what was intended." "Further, there is an allusion to examine in the word ״לָהֶם״. Also, the verse's saying <b>אִישׁ</b> — for it should not have needed to say anything other than ״וְיִקְחוּ שֶׂה לְבֵית״ and so forth. However, it intends according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory (Tanhuma), on the verse ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי תְּרוּמָה״, and this is their language: The Holy One, blessed be He, said: take Me with you. And this is what it says here, for the reason that since this is the first mitzvah, He informed them of the attainment of the mitzvah — that in performing it, HaShem's Shekhinah would dwell upon him, in the sod of (Deuteronomy 28:10) ״וְרָאוּ כָּל עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ כִּי שֵׁם ה׳ נִקְרָא עָלֶיךָ״. They also said that the word ״מִצְוָה״ contains the Name הוי״ה — half by א״ת ב״ש and half by ordinary order (Tikkunei Zohar, Tikkun 29). And this is what it means by <b>וְיִקְחוּ לָהֶם אִישׁ</b> — meaning, according to their statement, of blessed memory (Sotah 42a), ״אִישׁ״ — אִישׁ means none other than the Holy One, blessed be He, for in this mitzvah they would take for themselves a great and mighty taking, and He is the אִישׁ who is our Elohim, our King." "<b>And I considered</b> giving attention to why HaShem did not command in the Pesach of Egypt concerning the impure, the uncircumcised, and the children of the foreigner, that they should not eat of the Pesach. It is true that the warning concerning the impure, according to what we wrote in the portion of ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״ (Numbers 19:2), has a correct reason in the matter. But the uncircumcised and the children of the foreigner — why did HaShem not command them? And behold, according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory, Israel were warned concerning both: concerning circumcision, as stated in the words of the Prophets (Ezekiel 16:6) ״וָאֶרְאֵךְ מִתְבּוֹסֶסֶת בְּדָמָיִךְ״, and our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Exodus Rabbah 19:6): the blood of Pesach and the blood of circumcision; and concerning the child of the foreigner, our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Exodus Rabbah 16) on the verse ״מִשְׁכוּ״ and so forth — מִשְׁכוּ יְדֵיכֶם מֵעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה, withdraw your hands from idolatry. Behold, He warned them about this matter, for before the mitzvah of Pesach they needed to depart from the category of idolaters and enter into the category of Israel. Yet all this does not suffice for our difficulty, for why did these things not come in the words of HaShem at the core of the mitzvah?" "<b>And behold,</b> at the end of the portion, after it arranged Israel's exodus from Egypt, the section of ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַפָּסַח״ and so forth, ״כָּל בֶּן נֵכָר״ and so forth, ״וְכָל עֶבֶד״ and so forth, was stated. And I saw the words of Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, of blessed memory, who wrote that this section was stated for the Pesach of future generations and adds other mitzvot and so on. And if he intended to say that בֶּן נֵכָר and עָרֵל are additional mitzvot for the Pesach of future generations, his words here are nullified according to the words of our Sages, of blessed memory, and also from what the verse concludes there in the section of עָרֵל and בֶּן נֵכָר, saying (Exodus 12:50) ״וַיַּעֲשׂוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר״ and so forth. For if this section were stated for the Pesach of future generations, it should not have said ״וַיַּעֲשׂוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה״ and so forth, which implies that Israel did everything stated in the section. Also, what he said — that it intends the Pesach that they performed in the wilderness of Sinai — is an unsubstantiated assertion, and who would listen to him? And there is no need even to accept his words in this, although the Rav, of blessed memory, was careful with his own flour and does not dispute the words of our Sages except in a matter that has no bearing on the future — even so, in what stands before us too he disputes without the depth of compelling proof and the like." "<b>And I saw</b> the words of our Sages, of blessed memory, in the Mekhilta, who said, and this is their language: ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַפָּסַח״ (Exodus 12:43) — the verse speaks of the Pesach of Egypt and the Pesach of future generations; these are the words of Rabbi Yoshiah. Rabbi Yonatan says: the verse speaks of the Pesach of Egypt. I have only the Pesach of Egypt; from where do I derive the Pesach of future generations? Scripture teaches, saying ״כְּכָל חֻקֹּתָיו וְגוֹ׳ יַעֲשׂוּ אֹתוֹ״ (Numbers 9:3), against their will. Behold, according to all the opinions of the Tannaim the verse speaks of the Pesach of Egypt, and the words of Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra are uprooted and are as though they never were." "And according to this it is possible that the verse alludes in its saying <b>וְיִקְחוּ</b> — meaning, that besides the mitzvah of circumcision and the mitzvah of their withdrawal from idolatry, which is the aspect of בֶּן נֵכָר, that HaShem commanded, He further added to say ״וְיִקְחוּ״ and so forth. And the verse's saying <b>לָהֶם</b> here alludes to the exclusion of the בֶּן נֵכָר and the עָרֵל, that they would not be able to eat of the Pesach, as the matter comes explained in the prophecy of ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַפָּסַח״ (Exodus 12:43)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "The Isaiah citation appears to be verse 3:10 by its content (אמרו צדיק כי טוב); the address (ישעיהו ג:י) is as given in the Hebrew text.", 5 "The acronym א״ת ב״ש refers to א״ת ב״ג (atbash), the standard letter-substitution cipher; transliterated as is to preserve the Or HaChaim's exact Hebrew forms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:32.179046038Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}